EXT. SALOON ROOFTOP, DEAD MAN'S CROSSING - SUNSET

A flat saloon roof with a rickety rain barrel at one corner. A church bell hangs from a crooked post nearby. The street below is still. Warm light on dust.

SHERIFF OBADIAH TRELAWNEY-KANE (50s, white mustache, a star pinned to a faded vest) sits on the barrel playing a harmonica. Across from him, DEPUTY JEDEDIAH "JED" RUSSO-ABIOLA (20s, hat tilted) eats beans from a tin. A woman in a gingham dress, MAGDALENA SOLIS-FAIRWEATHER (30s, a basket on her arm), hangs laundry on a line between two chimneys. Near the edge, FINNEGAN "FINN" O'HALLORAN-DIOP (40s, drover's hat pulled low) leans against the bell post, whittling.

OBADIAH
(to Jed)
Quiet day.

JED
Too quiet.

MAGDALENA
(to all of them)
Anyone want pie?

FINN
(muttering)
Pie, she says.

OBADIAH
I'll take pie, Maggie.

JED
I'll take two.

MAGDALENA
Then you can come get it.

OBADIAH
Come get it, Jed.

JED
Why me?

FINN
Because you're the youngest, son.

JED
I'm not --

OBADIAH ^
Quiet.

FINN
Hear that?

All four go still. Far off, hoofbeats. A hundred horses. Then a thousand. The church bell hums on its post.

MAGDALENA
Obadiah?

OBADIAH
Everyone down.

JED
But sir --

FINN
Down!

They drop behind the parapet. From the end of the street, a dark mass rolls in: a cattle drive, massive and wild, tearing down Main Street toward them.

JED
(laughing, relieved)
It's only cattle.

OBADIAH
Look at the riders.

Behind the stampede, a line of masked men with torches lopes into town.

MAGDALENA
(whispering)
Lord help us.

FINN
(to himself)
Not today.

Obadiah rises, tucks the harmonica into his vest, and draws his revolver.
